# Roost has no entry in GitHub's linguist, so `.roost` files render as plain text — which is how
# the most interesting files in this repository end up looking like the least interesting ones.
#
# Kotlin is the closest borrowed grammar, and the resemblance is not a coincidence of syntax alone:
# a Roost declaration is a name, a typed parameter list and a braced body,
#
#   note Reservation(seller: Key, buyer: Key, expires: Height) { ... }
#
# which is exactly the shape Kotlin highlights, down to `name: Type` and `//` comments. What it
# gets wrong is small and visible: Roost's clause names (`spend`, `recover`, `by`, `after`, `out`)
# are not Kotlin keywords and stay unhighlighted, and `check` blocks read as ordinary calls. None
# of that misleads — an unhighlighted word is not a wrongly-coloured one.
#
# This is a **display** choice and nothing more. `roost check` is what decides whether a file is
# valid, and it has never heard of Kotlin.
*.roost linguist-language=Kotlin

# The artwork is data, not source. Without this the repository is 102 PNGs and GitHub's language
# bar describes it by what it counts rather than by what it is for.
pon/art/*.png linguist-generated=true

# A collection too large to carry its digests inline publishes them as `pon/d.bin`, a flat run of
# 32-byte BLAKE2b digests (`spec/asset-collection-v0.md` §3.3.1). This repository's collection has
# a hundred members and correctly keeps them inline, so no such file is committed here — the rule
# is written down anyway, because a fork that runs `tools/doc.py digests --table` commits a binary
# that GitHub would otherwise count as source and try to diff.
pon/d.bin binary

# A collection whose per-piece detail will not fit in the document publishes it as `pon/i.json`,
# the `items` array on its own (`spec/asset-collection-v0.md` §3.4.1). This one has no `items` at
# all — its pieces differ only in the picture `item.image` already templates — so again no such
# file is committed here, and again the rule is written down for the fork that runs `tools/doc.py
# items --external`.
#
# **Not `binary`, and not for the language bar.** It is text, it diffs, and JSON is already data
# to linguist rather than a language, so it stays out of the bar unaided. `linguist-generated`
# is about **review**: the file is written by the tool from the member that would otherwise be
# inline, so a pull request that regenerates it is thousands of lines in which nothing was
# decided. What was decided is the `b2` and the `bytes` in `pon/c.json`, two lines away, and
# they are what a reviewer should be looking at.
pon/i.json linguist-generated=true
x/art/*.png linguist-generated=true
x/d.bin binary
x/i.json linguist-generated=true
